WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.160
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
gents. This is Tim Feriss. Welcome to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.520
episode of The Tim Feriss Show, and it is my&nbsp;
pleasure today to have Willoughby Britton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.640
PhD on the podcast. She is a clinical&nbsp;
psychologist, and associate professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.760
of psychiatry and human behavior at Brown&nbsp;
University Medical School, and the director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:38.200
of Brown's Clinical and Affective Neuroscience&nbsp;
Laboratory, and that is affective with an A. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.360
clinical and neuroscience research investigates&nbsp;
the effects of contemplative practices, i.e.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.040
meditation, or certainly one example being&nbsp;
meditation on the brain and body in the treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.240
of mood disorders, trauma, and other conditions.
She's especially interested in which practices are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.240
best or worst suited for which types of people, or&nbsp;
conditions, and why. She's probably best known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:04.440
her research on adverse effects, why they happen,&nbsp;
and how to mitigate them, and we'll be doing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:11.040
very deep dive on this, certainly. Dr. Britton is&nbsp;
the founder of Cheetah House, as in the large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.960
very fast cat, Cheetah House, a nonprofit&nbsp;
organization that provides evidence-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.720
information, and support for meditators&nbsp;
in distress, as well as meditation safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.800
trainings to providers, and organizations. You can&nbsp;
find Cheetah House on Twitter @CheetahHouseOrg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:34.040
Facebook, you can find @cheetahhouse.org, and on&nbsp;
Instagram, @cheetah.house. Dr. Britton, thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.360
for making the time today. Nice to see you.
Willoughby Britton: Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.120
you so much for having me.
Tim Ferriss: There are many reasons that I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:47.680
to have a conversation with you, and one certainly&nbsp;
was due to my own experience at a silent retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.600
and having a flooding of traumatic memories, and&nbsp;
everything that that led to it was overwhelming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:01.000
to an extent that I could not have imagined, and&nbsp;
certainly had not been prepared for. Secondly is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.520
a mutual acquaintance, I won't mention his name,&nbsp;
because he's one of the more private people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.840
the world, so I would always want to double-check.&nbsp;
Had suggested that we talk, because there might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.800
some mapping, or some similarities at least&nbsp;
between meditation-related adverse events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.640
and how symptoms present some of the dissociation,&nbsp;
et cetera, that you can observe in some people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.920
and some of the adverse effects that you can see,&nbsp;
some persistent effects after psychedelic use.&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.040
So, eventually, we'll get to that.&nbsp;
But let's start at the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:41.248
or at least with the genesis story of sorts. How&nbsp;
did you first become interested in meditation?&nbsp;

00:02:41.248 --> 00:02:45.440
Willoughby Britton: So I think, like many people,&nbsp;
I had a life crisis that precipitated the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:53.480
for something really different in my life. One of&nbsp;
my childhood friends committed suicide when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.480 --> 00:03:01.320
in college, the summer between my junior&nbsp;
and senior year. And it was devastating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:08.560
and completely dysregulating to see a&nbsp;
person just disappear from the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.640
It wasn't in my repertoire of possibilities&nbsp;
that somebody could just not be there anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:21.160
so I needed some — so, I had probably diagnosable&nbsp;
PTSD at that point, and I really didn't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:29.960
take medication. I was only 20. And my dad&nbsp;
sent me A Path with Heart by Jack Kornfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:37.240
and that became my Bible for the next, probably,&nbsp;
decade, and so for those of you who don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.560
Jack Kornfield is a very famous meditation&nbsp;
teacher, and he's written many books. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:49.520
particular one, like many of them start off with&nbsp;
just what the mind can do, and how to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:57.840
it, and it just is very practical, a practical way&nbsp;
to manage all of the emotions that were happening.&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.520
And it was great, because it was allowing them&nbsp;
to be there too, it wasn't suppressing them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.480
Ironically, that book, that particular book that&nbsp;
he wrote, I only got about halfway through it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.760
because the whole second half I just could not&nbsp;
relate to, because it was like the spiritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.960
roller coaster, and like Kundalini awakenings.&nbsp;
And I was like, "I don't know what that is, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.760
I'll just put that down. Just take what you need,&nbsp;
whatever." And then that came back. Fast-forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:32.920
15 years, I was like, "Oh, right. That second&nbsp;
half of that book, let me go back and read that."&nbsp;

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.440
But I'm getting ahead of myself. So, going&nbsp;
into graduate school, I was also very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:45.280
very interested in consciousness always, and&nbsp;
always interested in just, what are we doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:54.680
here? What's going on? And what's looking out of&nbsp;
my eyes, and just very curious. And so, that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:03.760
already happening. And then I had this experience&nbsp;
and it culminated in my graduate career looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:11.560
at altered states of consciousness in a clinical&nbsp;
psychology program at the University of Arizona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.040
which happens to be, at the time, the only place&nbsp;
in the world that had a consciousness study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:23.400
center. So, I was doing that and actually,&nbsp;
my professional life, I actually studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:29.840
near-death experiences for my master's thesis.
And my own personal trauma, and meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:36.480
practice was something I was doing on my personal&nbsp;
time. It was not part of my scholarship. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:43.280
think at some point during graduate school, those&nbsp;
two lives were so divided that I needed to combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:50.960
them just to be like an integrated human being.
And so for my dissertation, I did a study on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:57.120
effects of mindfulness training on sleep, and the&nbsp;
reason — I was working in a sleep lab, and sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:04.120
is, it's a legal altered state of consciousness,&nbsp;
and it's linked with every type of possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:09.520
health issue. And so, if you want to study altered&nbsp;
states of consciousness, it's a good one to study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:14.040
because it's, you're not going to have — it's&nbsp;
not like psychedelics that get banned. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.680
does it. It happens every day.
Tim Ferriss: Hard to ban sleep.&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.640
Willoughby Britton: So it was a good one to&nbsp;
choose. And at the time, this is around 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.600
there really wasn't great research.&nbsp;
A lot of the meditation research was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.640
on self-reports. There wasn't a lot of brain&nbsp;
data available, and so I was going to be one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:42.520
of the first people to record overnight sleep&nbsp;
polysomnography, so this is brain-based sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:46.600
and prove that meditation was improving&nbsp;
sleep because, of course, it was. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:55.600
it's relaxing, et cetera. So, that was my&nbsp;
dissertation, and I spent like 200 nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:04.120
watching people sleep. And at the end of the&nbsp;
day, the hypothesis was not at all what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:11.800
expected. So we actually found that no matter&nbsp;
how you measured arousal, cortical arousal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:17.920
brain arousal, meditation was associated with&nbsp;
increasing it, not decreasing it, so it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:26.280
causing insomnia in people. And we looked at&nbsp;
percentage of slow-wave sleep that went down.&nbsp;

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:33.520
We went, and looked at how fast the brainwaves&nbsp;
were. Those went up, and the correlation was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:41.280
with the amount of practice was 0.8. So no matter&nbsp;
where you went, there it was, and this is my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:49.440
first confession is that I didn't publish the&nbsp;
data. I was like an evangelist for meditation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:55.800
because this is something that had really helped&nbsp;
me, and I really wanted to use my science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:02.920
and my position as a scientist to promote&nbsp;
this thing, and so I didn't publish the data.&nbsp;

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:08.640
At some point, when I came to Brown to&nbsp;
do my residency, a couple of years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.800
I was on a meditation retreat, and I&nbsp;
mentioned the data that I had found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:19.240
which is basically that it was causing cortical&nbsp;
arousal, and insomnia. And the meditation teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:25.600
sort of chastised me and said, "I don't know why&nbsp;
all you clinical psychologists are always trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.880
to make meditation into a relaxation technique.&nbsp;
Everyone knows that if you meditate enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.520
you stop sleeping." And I was like, "What?"
Tim Ferriss: Bulletin!&nbsp;

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:42.480
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. And so that was&nbsp;
like a wake-up call, literally. And I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:51.120
"Okay. Well, what other assumptions are we&nbsp;
making?" I was definitely pretty emblematic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.400
a lot of people who go into meditation research,&nbsp;
or the average meditator, which is like, I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:04.640
really know my Buddhism, I didn't really know my&nbsp;
history. I just signed up for whatever was around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:11.200
and believed everything that people said, and the&nbsp;
way that it was marketed as a health promotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:17.080
tonic. I believed all that, and I didn't do&nbsp;
my homework, which is pretty terrible as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:24.880
meditation researcher. And so I was like, "Okay.&nbsp;
It looks like I need to do some history homework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:30.880
and really look at the background of what kinds&nbsp;
of assumptions we're making as health providers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:37.280
and really look at the translation process&nbsp;
of how these practices, where they came from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.480
what kinds of changes they made, what&nbsp;
was the journey like to get here into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:49.320
the West, and really unpack that process."
And then the second question was, "Well, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:57.160
other little nuggets of knowledge do these&nbsp;
meditation teachers have that they're not telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:05.120
us?" And there's a follow-on part of the story,&nbsp;
which is when I came to Brown to do my clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.400
residency, you have to do a year of internship&nbsp;
to see clients, basically to get your license.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:17.960
I worked at an inpatient psychiatric hospital,&nbsp;
and during that one year, there were two yogis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:27.280
who came off retreat, completely psychotic. And&nbsp;
I thought like, "Wow, two in one year is, it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.840
lot." It seemed like a lot to me. So I went back&nbsp;
to the same teacher and I said, "Well, have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:39.720
ever seen this before?" And I remember distinctly&nbsp;
that there was no actual verbal reaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:47.840
but there was this look of like, "Oh, shit."
And I was like, "Oh, oh, wow. There is a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:54.920
here. There is a knowledge that this can happen,&nbsp;
and meditation teachers are sitting on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:59.600
just a ton of information that needs to be mined."&nbsp;
And as a meditation researcher, I was like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:07.160
we need to start asking these questions." So that&nbsp;
whole story is the beginning of the Varieties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:14.360
of Contemplative Experience study, which was&nbsp;
probably the simplest study that I've ever done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:19.640
the hardest, but simplest. And that it was&nbsp;
basically just going to meditation centers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:25.680
and teachers, and saying, "What types of&nbsp;
challenges have you observed in your students?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.880
How do you make sense of them? What are they? How&nbsp;
do you interpret them? And then very practically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:42.160
what do you do about them?" Very straightforward&nbsp;
set of questions, and that took 10 years. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.440
3,000 pages of qualitative data, and I don't even&nbsp;
know how many papers we've written. We're still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:58.520
writing papers based on that data. I think we're&nbsp;
at over 10 now, so it's really just a wormhole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:02.800
which we're going to go down together.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, we are going to go arm-in-arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:07.720
down the wormhole. So let me pause for a second.&nbsp;
I want to make a promise to folks, and I'll give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.440
you a heads-up also, we will come back to the&nbsp;
near-death experiences. I'm going to leave that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:18.280
a Scooby Snack at the end for people, so I do want&nbsp;
to talk about that. However, I want to dig into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:25.280
the sleep for one second, because if people stop&nbsp;
sleeping, period, full stop, eventually they die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:35.400
right? So the restorative functions of sleep, as&nbsp;
it is still poorly understood, but are critical to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:42.400
human functioning, and survival. How would you&nbsp;
explain, if it is indeed true, that if people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:47.320
meditate enough, they stop sleeping, or they&nbsp;
start sleeping very minimally? How would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:54.400
explain that? Is it that the meditation practice&nbsp;
itself provokes a state that is restorative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:01.720
and therefore the sleep is less necessary,&nbsp;
or is there something else that you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:06.600
put as the front-runner for explaining that?
Willoughby Britton: We can point readers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:14.560
various papers. There is a paper that was called&nbsp;
"Awakening Is Not a Metaphor." I had to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:15.600
hard to keep that title.
Tim Ferriss: That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.920
pretty good. That's pretty good.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:23.840
a review of different studies across different&nbsp;
types of methodologies, looking at how meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:32.040
affects the wake-sleep system, and it's not&nbsp;
as straightforward as you stop sleeping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:40.440
or you sleep less. It's going to be dependent on&nbsp;
many factors. And I will say that in the study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:47.200
that I was just talking about, my dissertation&nbsp;
study, we found a non-monotonic, non-linear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:56.200
relationship. So if people who are meditating&nbsp;
less than half an hour, and less than every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:05.280
they actually had an increase in sleep quality.&nbsp;
But people who are meditating more than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:10.480
that's when it sort of switched and turned&nbsp;
into, they were prone to being more awake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:16.480
more insomnia, and more cortical arousal, so&nbsp;
it's sort of biphasic, nonlinear relationship.&nbsp;

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:24.147
So, just to recap, it's not going&nbsp;
to give you insomnia, probably,&nbsp;

00:14:24.147 --> 00:14:27.120
Tim Ferriss: Automatically, yes. Because&nbsp;
when we talk about meditation, it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:30.481
"Oh, you do meditation?" It's kind of like,&nbsp;
"Oh, you do sports," or, "You do exercise."&nbsp;

00:14:30.481 --> 00:14:31.920
Willoughby Britton: Right.
Tim Ferriss: It's like, well, there are a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.760
different parameters, and types, and so on.
Willoughby Britton: Right. And I think one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.520
the other papers that I wrote, which is called,&nbsp;
"Can Mindfulness Be Too Much of a Good Thing?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:47.400
Introduces this idea of that there's, at a small&nbsp;
amount, or an optimal amount can do exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:56.000
the hype, and the promotions, and the advertising&nbsp;
says. There is a lot of truth in the advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:05.720
and the positive reports. But there's another&nbsp;
side of the story which often comes with sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:12.600
higher doses. So, one of the ways to understand&nbsp;
this particular finding, and actually a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:20.840
of the other ones that are on the hyperarousal&nbsp;
spectrum would be, if you think about meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:33.600
as a attention-enhancing product, it gets often&nbsp;
compared with, or marketed alongside Ritalin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:43.960
and Modafinil, and coffee, and caffeine, and all&nbsp;
these stimulants that help attention. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:50.286
if you think about meditation as one of those,&nbsp;
I mean, think about what happens when you —&nbsp;

00:15:50.286 --> 00:15:51.880
Tim Ferriss: Spiritual Modafinil,&nbsp;
which is an anti-narcolepsy drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:54.240
for people who don't recognize it.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. If you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.240 --> 00:16:00.680
about any of the consequences, and many of&nbsp;
us have personal experience with a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:05.360
these substances, taking too many of them,&nbsp;
taking too much. Just drink too much coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.960
what happens? You have a panic attack, and you&nbsp;
can't sleep. You take amphetamines, or cocaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:19.680
or whatever the stimulant is, you take enough of&nbsp;
it, and it's going to be on the fear spectrum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.680
And some of them, if you take enough of it, it's&nbsp;
going to be on the psychosis spectrum, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:31.320
if you have tendencies in that direction. So,&nbsp;
it doesn't seem like a huge leap to think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:37.120
here's another attention-enhancing product that,&nbsp;
with certain people and certain types of doses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:42.280
it's going to lead to a hyperarousal syndrome, and&nbsp;
that's pretty much exactly what we're seeing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:46.840
that is one of the potential mechanisms.
Tim Ferriss: So, what I might do for folks who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:53.920
just don't have the personal context on me that I&nbsp;
alluded to earlier is, I'll tell if it's okay with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.920 --> 00:17:00.520
you, a bit of my story, which in some ways led to&nbsp;
this conversation, and certainly, it sounds like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:06.240
based on what we were discussing before recording,&nbsp;
that people have mentioned this to you. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:13.120
it's been a match made for many reasons, so I&nbsp;
want to give folks sort of a contrast by giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:19.480
present day, and then the story of what happened&nbsp;
to me at my silent retreat, which was a Vipassana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:26.080
retreat. So if we look at, for instance, the&nbsp;
last two weeks, I have meditated twice a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:30.320
20 minutes a day, very straightforward&nbsp;
transcendental meditation. And there's lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:36.560
wacky weird stuff if you go deep enough and weird&nbsp;
enough with TM. But if you're taking it for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:43.240
off-the-shelf offering, and following the basic&nbsp;
guidelines, I find it incredibly therapeutic.&nbsp;

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:50.040
