00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:27.920
is pulling from an interview with you:
"With a more clear definition of adverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:32.400
effects in hand, Willoughby and her team set out&nbsp;
to research the true prevalence of life-impacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:35.480
adverse effects following meditation. The&nbsp;
first surprising result was that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:39.360
epidemiological sample indicated nearly half of&nbsp;
the US population has meditated at least once."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:44.800
I'm sure that's higher now. "Of these people,&nbsp;
six to 14 percent had lasting negative effects."&nbsp;

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:48.560
So that's what I wanted to bring up, a number&nbsp;
that closely mirrors the adverse effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:54.240
psychotherapy, five to 13 percent. Is&nbsp;
that roughly accurate, would you say?&nbsp;

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:59.280
Willoughby Britton: Frequency research is&nbsp;
very challenging, and so I do think that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:07.240
epidemiological study is probably the most valid&nbsp;
research, but somehow the data that they're citing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:11.300
is actually from combining two different studies.
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.&nbsp;

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:15.360
Willoughby Britton: So the epidemiological&nbsp;
study, the denominator was anyone who's ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:24.640
meditated even once. So that's the lowest common&nbsp;
denominator, so the lowest possible chance. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:31.520
within that study we found that people who had&nbsp;
tried meditation even once, half of them would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:42.240
have at least one negative effect. And it could&nbsp;
be extremely brief, no big deal. More concerning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:48.840
is that 10 percent, so one in 10 people who&nbsp;
had tried meditation even once, would have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:53.600
meditation-related adverse effect that was&nbsp;
associated with impairment in functioning.&nbsp;

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:59.760
And so we can go down the rabbit hole of&nbsp;
definitions if you want, but I think that for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:07.720
having a negative experience during meditation,&nbsp;
which we call negative valence, I don't consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:12.840
that an adverse effect. I think that if you don't&nbsp;
have some kind of negative experience in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:18.000
meditation at some point you're probably sleeping,&nbsp;
because it's not a warm bath for the mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:23.320
it can be challenging. So I'm not worried about&nbsp;
that part, but I'm more worried about if it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:27.920
a negative impact on your life and functioning&nbsp;
off the cushion, then I'm like, okay, that's when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:35.000
we need to start talking about whether there's&nbsp;
maybe alternatives. And so one in 10 is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:41.920
the number that is jumping out of that paper.
The other paper, which was a clinical trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:49.240
that I did, much smaller trial, very selected&nbsp;
sample of people who have clinical to subclinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:55.040
anxiety and depression, that came up with&nbsp;
the five to 15 percent, which is still, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:59.000
correlate with each other, but yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So what are some things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:06.560
that we can do to risk mitigate in terms of, and&nbsp;
you can pick whichever makes sense to start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.560 --> 00:38:14.200
but training for instructors, or monitoring. I'm&nbsp;
actually pretty interested in the monitoring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:20.360
I'm interested in all of it. And then&nbsp;
off-ramps, what to do if somebody does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.360 --> 00:38:25.160
have a break, I suppose, like I did. I wouldn't&nbsp;
necessarily describe it as a psychotic break,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:32.800
but I felt out of control. I felt like I would&nbsp;
not be able to function normally had I returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:39.460
to normal life without some intervention. What&nbsp;
can we do to make things safer, I suppose?&nbsp;

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:45.280
Willoughby Britton: I think it helps to start&nbsp;
with, we created a taxonomy of 59 categories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:52.440
of meditation-related challenges, and it&nbsp;
wasn't until many years later that I went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:59.560
through the somatic experiencing trauma training&nbsp;
that I started to actually see a lot of those 59&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:06.800
categories in terms of trauma symptoms, and in&nbsp;
terms of symptoms of the way that the body tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:15.880
to react to extreme stress. And that these are&nbsp;
not necessarily so altered states, but things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:23.160
that might be somewhat recognizable to people.
So one of the ways that I think is the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:29.680
efficient way, because we can train meditation&nbsp;
teachers and be like, I have online courses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:34.280
where you're like, they have quizzes on the&nbsp;
59 categories, and there's all this content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:41.000
that they need to learn. But I actually think&nbsp;
that the best way to do it is to train each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:48.360
individual person to be like, Tim, how do you&nbsp;
know when you're getting outside of your window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:56.400
of tolerance? How do you know when you're feeling&nbsp;
"out of control?" What is your indicator for that?&nbsp;

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:05.880
Tim Ferriss: It's when I seem incapable, or&nbsp;
maybe when I am incapable, of stopping very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:13.760
high-speed negative rumination, I suppose. It&nbsp;
would be perseverating on either a real event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:22.080
like a memory, or a perceived threat, like a&nbsp;
hypothetical worst case scenario, and I just loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:28.920
and loop and loop and loop. And I'm furthermore&nbsp;
aware of the looping, right? There's part of me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:35.040
as the observer that's like, holy shit, I can't&nbsp;
believe I seem incapable of stopping this. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:40.280
it's not productive, I know it's not helping&nbsp;
me, and yet it just goes and goes and goes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:48.777
That would be my canary in the coal mine, or&nbsp;
maybe a flock of canaries in the coal mine.&nbsp;

00:40:48.777 --> 00:40:52.040
Willoughby Britton: So is there anything&nbsp;
that is an earlier indicator than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:55.560
before you get there?
Tim Ferriss: I would say it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.560 --> 00:41:02.000
more I've realized which factors can contribute&nbsp;
to that. It's something for me that comes on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:09.880
so this may be a dissatisfying answer, but it's&nbsp;
something that can come on really suddenly, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:18.920
I would say that overcaffeination, lack of sleep,&nbsp;
fasting, there are a handful of things that seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:25.640
more likely to precipitate this type of experience&nbsp;
than other things. It doesn't happen all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:34.480
but I would say probably, if I were to try&nbsp;
to back into what I described, I'm sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:44.080
it starts with, and I'm sure it started, I have&nbsp;
to imagine, at my Vipassana retreat with having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:52.440
bouts of this come up and then trying to suppress&nbsp;
it, I'm sure it took some form like that. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:57.360
have to imagine it was something like that.
Willoughby Britton: So, just to quid pro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.360 --> 00:42:02.480
quo, I mean, I have lots of them, but&nbsp;
I think my mouth gets dry, is like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:06.820
indication of sympathetic activation.
Tim Ferriss: Very interesting, yeah.&nbsp;

00:42:06.820 --> 00:42:15.400
Willoughby Britton: I get really hot, and when&nbsp;
I'm really about to, a strong stressor for me is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:21.880
when my visual field starts to get wonky,&nbsp;
and that's when the dissociation sets in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:26.160
Things get a little spacey, like that.
So if this was a meditation class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:35.480
I would have everyone go around and talk about&nbsp;
their own personal indicators. Because no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:40.000
how much research we do, those indicators&nbsp;
are, there's going to be a lot of overlap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:43.640
but there are also going to be some individual&nbsp;
differences. And really what matters is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:49.680
the person knows what their indicators are,&nbsp;
they're like, okay, my system is stressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:58.160
don't keep doing what you're doing that's making&nbsp;
this happen, let's dial it back. And unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.160 --> 00:43:03.760
the response is often, well just keep going,&nbsp;
just keep meditating, keep doing whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:11.080
you're doing, at some point there's going to be&nbsp;
a breakthrough, but instead it just gets worse.&nbsp;

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:17.240
So that is more of the angle that I'm taking,&nbsp;
is teaching everybody what their indicator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:21.800
lights are. Just like you don't get in&nbsp;
a car and drive until you know, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:28.560
the gas meter's here, this is the tire pressure,&nbsp;
you have these indicator lights, and we have them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:34.440
we have indicator lights, and we all need&nbsp;
to be trained on what they are individually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:39.480
And that will keep you a lot safer when&nbsp;
you know what your indicator lights are.&nbsp;

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:47.320
I think one of the things that undermines that is&nbsp;
this fascination and romanticization of altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.320 --> 00:43:54.080
states. And you can think of altered&nbsp;
states as a deviation from baseline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.080 --> 00:44:03.320
a deviation from homeostasis, which is, in trauma&nbsp;
speak, that is dysregulation. An altered state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:05.560
is not necessarily something that you want to be,&nbsp;
it's not necessarily a good idea for someone who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:05.560 --> 00:44:12.160
trying to stay stable. And so just having people&nbsp;
be able to track that and, okay, that thing, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:19.600
indicator light, is on, what typically helps that?&nbsp;
And go do that. I need to go lift some weights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.600 --> 00:44:26.000
I need to go dial back and I need to skip the&nbsp;
coffee this afternoon, or maybe eat something.&nbsp;

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:33.560
By the way, the fasting thing is really, really&nbsp;
interesting. We had a lot of information in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.560 --> 00:44:37.960
varieties project about diet, but we really&nbsp;
hadn't done anything with it, and actually one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:46.600
of my students this year has just started to delve&nbsp;
into the diet data and is finding that it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:53.680
making a difference. That a lot of people have&nbsp;
reported that when you go on meditation retreats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.680 --> 00:44:59.280
because they tend to be vegetarian and&nbsp;
they tend to feed you very light food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:05.920
sometimes not even, sometimes there's no dinner.
And so it's not fasting so much, but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:13.200
definitely like you're going off of your, like my&nbsp;
personal high protein diet, I get very unregulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:20.440
and floaty and ungrounded when I go on retreats&nbsp;
and I really want to have heavy food. And so a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:20.440 --> 00:45:28.680
lot of people found that to be a factor, and found&nbsp;
that actually eating really high fat protein meat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.680 --> 00:45:37.960
is a remedy for the problems. And this is also&nbsp;
recommended within Tibetan medicine, and old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:45.160
Buddhist textual reports will also report eating&nbsp;
meat and heavy foods, oils can also be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:52.880
that can help bring people down and more grounded.
And when we interviewed the teachers as teachers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.880 --> 00:45:58.800
which was the second part of the study, there&nbsp;
was really not a lot of consensus about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:03.480
was a problem, what wasn't a problem, what was&nbsp;
a risk factor, what wasn't. But there were two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:09.160
things, and one was when the person stops&nbsp;
sleeping and when the person stops eating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:14.200
that's when you intervene. So those were the&nbsp;
two things that people, all teachers, mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:18.000
as being something that they worry about.
Tim Ferriss: So let me hop in for a second just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:27.160
to mention something that I found in the process&nbsp;
of doing homework for this. This was actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:36.560
Harper's piece about you and your team, and it&nbsp;
discussed something that I've also seen, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:43.680
does not really get discussed, so I'm bringing&nbsp;
it up very deliberately, post certain potent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:47.520
psychedelic experiences. So this is something that&nbsp;
I've seen in both, well actually I should say now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:53.160
I've seen described here as an&nbsp;
adverse effect of meditation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:53.160 --> 00:47:01.440
possibly, for some folks, and also I've seen this&nbsp;
post-psychedelic. And this is, I'll just read:&nbsp;

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:05.400
"'Practicing letting go of concepts,' one&nbsp;
meditator told Britton, 'was sabotaging my mind's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.120
ability to lay down new memories and reinforce old&nbsp;
memories of simple things, like what words mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:13.000
what colors mean.'" What I'm going to underline&nbsp;
is what's coming next: "Meditators also reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:20.240
diminished emotions, both negative and positive.&nbsp;
'I had two young children,' another meditator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:23.920
said. 'I couldn't feel anything about them. I went&nbsp;
through all the routines, you know: the bedtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.920 --> 00:47:27.720
routine, getting them ready and kissing them and&nbsp;
all of that stuff, but there was no emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:34.480
connection. It was like I was dead.'"
So this anhedonia, let's call it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.480 --> 00:47:40.560
which interestingly can also be, for some people,&nbsp;
addressed with psychedelics, but what's less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:46.920
talked about is that it can be initiated in some&nbsp;
people by psychedelics, where they come out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:53.440
they're sort of numb to the world. People&nbsp;
think of the amygdala as this fear center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.440 --> 00:47:59.840
but it's not exactly a fear center. So maybe you&nbsp;
could just speak to this particular manifestation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:59.840 --> 00:48:04.760
of issue, because I've seen it in both camps.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah, so there's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:13.240
two different ways that this can happen with&nbsp;
meditation and psychedelics. With meditation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.240 --> 00:48:20.400
if you look at the neuroscience of meditation,&nbsp;
and there's lots of different kinds, but we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:27.920
just say concentration practice, you tend to see a&nbsp;
downregulation of the limbic system, the amygdala,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:36.680
and activation of the prefrontal cortex. So&nbsp;
attention system goes up, downregulates the limbic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:44.000
system. And again, this can be great, good things,&nbsp;
you regulate all your anxiety and depression.&nbsp;

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:51.480
But if you keep going, then you're going to see&nbsp;
a downregulation of not just negative emotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:57.240
but also positive ones. People forget that the&nbsp;
amygdala is a salience detector for all sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:04.040
things, positive and negative, and you're going&nbsp;
to see, if you continue to downregulate your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:11.880
limbic system with meditation, and you take&nbsp;
it far enough, then you will see a decrease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:22.800
in the intensity of all emotions, otherwise&nbsp;
known as anhedonia. According to [inaudible],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:29.027
that could also be something that could be&nbsp;
equanimity. So it depends on what you want.&nbsp;

00:49:29.027 --> 00:49:32.760
Tim Ferriss: Tomato, tomato, right?
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. Obviously the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:38.520
who didn't feel anything for her children, that&nbsp;
was not her goal. But I actually, that particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:43.840
quote that you're reading, I read that quote in a&nbsp;
conference and a Buddhist scholar jumped up at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:50.760
end and said, "Cutting attachments, affection&nbsp;
with your families, is exactly what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:56.400
supposed to be doing." And so I was like, "Okay,&nbsp;
well, we need to talk about what your goals are."&nbsp;

00:49:56.400 --> 00:50:01.080
Tim Ferriss: Thou doth protest too much.
Willoughby Britton: So with meditation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:08.560
there is overlap, so what's interesting about&nbsp;
that model, prefrontal cortex control over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:15.400
limbic system, is that that's also the&nbsp;
neuroscience of dissociation. So there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:22.080
are certain forms of meditation that mimic the&nbsp;
exact neurobiology of dissociation, so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:26.000
one way to end up in this place.
The other way, so I call that the low road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:30.080
because you're just basically just taking&nbsp;
everything down, getting calmer and calmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.080 --> 00:50:36.080
and calmer. The high road is the one we were&nbsp;
talking about earlier where you're training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.080 --> 00:50:40.240
your attention and everything's upregulating&nbsp;
and you're getting more and more and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:44.440
your thoughts are racing and you're having&nbsp;
flooding of traumatic experiences and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.440 --> 00:50:49.760
everything's getting heightened. And at some&nbsp;
point your system's like, "Whoa, this is too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:58.520
much," and you have this beautiful, adaptive,&nbsp;
natural breaker switch called dissociation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:01.200
that will basically take the heightened, it'll&nbsp;
basically shut down the limbic system again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:04.000
and you'll basically end up in the state, the&nbsp;
first state that I just talked about, where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:10.720
have a strong activation of the prefrontal cortex&nbsp;
and a downregulation of the limbic system. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:10.720 --> 00:51:18.360
so it's two different roads to the same place.
And so what's interesting, and the overlap with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:25.040
psychedelics, is that too much response that can&nbsp;
lead to dissociation, it doesn't always have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:25.040 --> 00:51:33.760
be negative. People can have a euphoric unitive&nbsp;
experience where they merge with the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:39.920
or they have just something just ecstatic kind&nbsp;
of experience, but their organism is like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:46.560
"That was too intense. That was too much for me.&nbsp;
I'm turning on the breaker." And they wake up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:46.560 --> 00:51:55.360
the morning and they feel dead, and they spend&nbsp;
years trying to recreate that awesome experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:56.480 --> 00:52:02.000
but they're not really listening to their&nbsp;
organism's message, which is saying, "That was too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:03.760
intense. Are you listening?" And so they go back.
And so we actually see a lot of people, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:09.560
a very interesting overlap at Cheetah House when&nbsp;
people come for help, we see the combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:17.480
psychedelics and meditation very often, a lot&nbsp;
of times in this scenario, where they go on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:23.960
meditation retreat, have an ecstatic experience,&nbsp;
or just a very intense experience, wake up feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:34.080
dead and anhedonic, and they think, "I've got&nbsp;
to sort of shock the system back into action and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:40.160
they go take a psychedelic." And that just makes&nbsp;
things worse because the system's like, "I've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:45.960
trying to tell you that this is too much and&nbsp;
now you're just really pushing the envelope."&nbsp;

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:52.320
In the end we also see the opposite, people who&nbsp;
have the initial experience on psychedelics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:52.320 --> 00:53:00.680
then they decide, "Well, I'm very dysregulated,"&nbsp;
and they get told by various authorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:04.960
"You should go on a meditation retreat,"&nbsp;
or, "You should meditate." Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.960 --> 00:53:09.547
at least the people who show up at Cheetah House,&nbsp;
the combination was not the answer for them.&nbsp;

00:53:09.547 --> 00:53:18.280
Tim Ferriss: Let me hop in for a second just&nbsp;
to ask a few follow-up questions on retreat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.280 --> 00:53:30.400
specifically. Are there any intensive retreats&nbsp;
that you recommend, or extended retreats? And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.400 --> 00:53:39.640
suppose the broader question is how can one&nbsp;
know where to practice and vet properly if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:39.640 --> 00:53:43.800
someone wants to do a meditation retreat?
And the reason I ask is, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:48.360
with psychedelics, which are still&nbsp;
largely underground at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:53.360
even though at some point hopefully there&nbsp;
will be an entire framework for administering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.360 --> 00:54:00.800
them reasonably safely to people who fit certain&nbsp;
criteria, if someone finds a facilitator who says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:06.280
"I've never had — no one under my care has ever&nbsp;
had a bad trip," that is a huge red flag because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:12.360
it means they're either deluding themselves,&nbsp;
they're lying, or they're really inexperienced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:20.920
Those are kind of the only options on the table&nbsp;
because you're effectively using nuclear power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:28.880
to change the plasticity of the mind, of course,&nbsp;
there are going to be adverse events, of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:28.880 --> 00:54:34.640
there are going to be outliers, and so you want&nbsp;
someone who has actually handled those cases.&nbsp;

00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:39.400
If you go to, to push my F1 analogy, if you go&nbsp;
to a racetrack, let's just say, and you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:45.480
to a track day, and the track owner says, "We've&nbsp;
never had any accident of any type on our track,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:49.880
that's a bad thing because someone's going to&nbsp;
have an accident, you want to make sure they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:54.120
protocols in place, they have experience, they&nbsp;
have the presence of mind to handle it calmly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.120 --> 00:55:00.720
et cetera, et cetera. So I could see that&nbsp;
applying also to vetting meditation retreats.&nbsp;

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:05.160
But putting myself in the position of someone&nbsp;
listening to this, they might say, "Holy shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:09.560
of the people who have tried meditation once,&nbsp;
X percentage of these persistent problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:16.440
this seems really, really dangerous, maybe I just&nbsp;
shouldn't meditate." So for those people, to maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:23.440
offset that a little bit, are there retreats&nbsp;
that you ever recommend? And how can someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.440 --> 00:55:27.920
vet if they're considering doing a retreat?
Willoughby Britton: Yeah, wow. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:35.000
I think that a lot of it has to do with&nbsp;
matching the goals to the person. So I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:41.267
want to necessarily rule out or recommend&nbsp;
any particular retreat across the board.&nbsp;

00:55:41.267 --> 00:55:43.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Willoughby Britton: I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:52.600
there are certain retreats that are pretty repeat&nbsp;
offenders, and those are ones that have high dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.600 --> 00:56:02.640
like 15 hours of meditation a day, no movement&nbsp;
practice. So often you see an alternation between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:12.040
walking and sitting, and sometimes there's&nbsp;
even yoga added. So more intense practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:12.040 --> 00:56:21.240
with no movement, and also not necessarily&nbsp;
tailored feedback from teachers. So I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.240 --> 00:56:28.000
be very careful before going on one of those.
And I think, just in general, there are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:33.080
different options for retreats these days, you&nbsp;
can do an afternoon retreat where it's only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.080 --> 00:56:38.240
couple hours, start there, titrate up, see&nbsp;
how it is. And you can do this at home, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:44.200
don't even have to necessarily spend the money,&nbsp;
you can just download an app and do that for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:52.600
day and see how you do. Titrate up and just do it&nbsp;
one, add a day at a time if you're worried about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.360 --> 00:56:56.640
rather than signing up for a 10-day retreat.
Tim Ferriss: There's something I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:56.640 --> 00:57:00.160
not mentioned before is that I've&nbsp;
done shorter meditation retreats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:00.160 --> 00:57:04.760
like two or three days, with no issues whatsoever.
So I just want to point that out. The other fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:09.360
detail that I want to mention, because I&nbsp;
think Spirit Rock runs a very good ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:18.840
and I think they're very well-formatted and they&nbsp;
do have safeguards in place that they explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:30.120
advise against fasting, and I violated that rule.&nbsp;
I overrode that, and also added the psychedelics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:36.020
which certainly I had not mentioned to&nbsp;
anyone until I had already capsized.&nbsp;

00:57:36.020 --> 00:57:44.600
Willoughby Britton: Yeah, this never used to be&nbsp;
an issue, but I know that people are doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:44.600 --> 00:57:52.720
that people are bringing psychedelics on retreats.&nbsp;
And I think a lot of the retreats, they have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.720 --> 00:58:01.680
manage a lot of people, and people are already&nbsp;
having challenging experiences, the regular kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.680 --> 00:58:09.520
of challenging experiences with meditation, and&nbsp;
so to have to manage people who are also taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.520 --> 00:58:15.143
psychedelics, that's a lot, it's not really&nbsp;
fair to a meditation retreat. So I think that —&nbsp;

00:58:15.143 --> 00:58:18.760
Tim Ferriss: No, it's going to be invisible&nbsp;
to them, for the most part, I'm sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:23.280
right? It's not going to be reported. Just like&nbsp;
people lie on their medical intakes about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:29.600
psychiatric medications they're taking if&nbsp;
they are wedded to taking psychedelics with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.600 --> 00:58:36.000
a facilitator. This happens all the time, people&nbsp;
misrepresent their health status because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:43.400
so vested in this last Hail Mary, "Obi-Wan Kenobi,&nbsp;
you're our final hope" solution panacea that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.400 --> 00:58:48.520
see in psychedelics, and I have to imagine&nbsp;
that also happens with meditation retreats.&nbsp;

00:58:48.520 --> 00:58:52.920
I should also say, I'd love for you to say a&nbsp;
bit more about repeat offenders. Are there any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.920 --> 00:59:01.520
other characteristics or format issues that&nbsp;
you see producing more problems than others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.520 --> 00:59:04.760
outside of what you already mentioned?
Willoughby Britton: So in terms of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.760 --> 00:59:09.318
retreat or meditation type, or in&nbsp;
terms of personal risk factors?&nbsp;

00:59:09.318 --> 00:59:12.880
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that seems to produce, the&nbsp;
retreat or meditation type that seemed to produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.880 --> 00:59:18.160
a higher volume of people with these issues.
Willoughby Britton: So I would say retreats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.160 --> 00:59:26.880
that recruit or are attracting a certain type&nbsp;
of meditator, which by the way, you fit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:33.196
bill pretty exactly. When I heard this story&nbsp;
I was like, wow, that's pretty emblematic of —&nbsp;

00:59:33.196 --> 00:59:36.760
Tim Ferriss: Textbook.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. Young male,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:36.760 --> 00:59:44.320
pretty educated, combining all sorts of tools,&nbsp;
fairly aggressive. We used to joke that one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:44.320 --> 00:59:52.160
the risk factors was zealotry, a kind of zealotry,&nbsp;
a zeal, so something like that. And so people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:58.880
there's a certain kind of, almost like military,&nbsp;
this is going to be a really, really hard retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:04.360
those types of retreats are a little bit&nbsp;
more high-risk. And I think there's also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.360 --> 01:00:10.600
combination of the person and the teacher/format.&nbsp;
And so for people who are — so one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:15.520
that we found that was really shocking in the&nbsp;
Varieties of Contemplative Experience study&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:21.720
is that on one hand, we expected to see people&nbsp;
who ran into problems, as people who had lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:28.240
problems in their lives. But when we actually&nbsp;
looked at the data, 75 percent had graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:36.120
degrees, MD, PhDs, JDs. These were CEOs of major&nbsp;
companies. These are super high-achieving people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.120 --> 01:00:39.760
and we were like, "This is so interesting. How&nbsp;
do we make sense of that?" And we're like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:45.320
right, being a high achiever is a risk factor."
Tim Ferriss: I was just about to say that drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.320 --> 01:00:51.140
addiction in the medical profession&nbsp;
is suicides off the charts. Right.&nbsp;

01:00:51.140 --> 01:00:54.560
Willoughby Britton: It's because these are&nbsp;
the kinds of people that you're like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.560 --> 01:00:59.840
we're going to sit and follow your breath," and&nbsp;
they're like, "Okay." They're the ones that show&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:59.840 --> 01:01:04.320
up early for the meditation and they're the&nbsp;
last ones to leave. They're the ones that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.320 --> 01:01:11.200
like — they follow instructions exactly, and they&nbsp;
would never modify the instructions for their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:15.280
benefit. That would not even occur to them.
Tim Ferriss: Unless they make it more intense.&nbsp;

01:01:15.280 --> 01:01:22.880
Willoughby Britton: This is kind of where&nbsp;
trauma comes in. If you've been trained to scan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:29.720
what are the expectations here? What are&nbsp;
the unspoken social rules that I need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:35.400
ace in order to not be punished? If that's&nbsp;
kind of your MO running in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:40.720
then we have all these people following&nbsp;
instructions exactly, not modifying them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:47.440
Basically, listening to an external authority&nbsp;
rather than their own internal compass, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:55.040
the recipe for disaster, so if you can interface&nbsp;
with, really, any type of meditation spiritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:55.040 --> 01:02:03.440
system with maintaining your inner compass, that's&nbsp;
going to be a recipe for a much better outcome.&nbsp;

01:02:03.440 --> 01:02:09.960
But not everybody can do that and&nbsp;
not all systems are tolerant of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:15.560
so I would also encourage — we've had lots of&nbsp;
trainings with meditation centers, trying to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.560 --> 01:02:23.960
able to be more flexible. So if somebody knows,&nbsp;
"I need to be able to leave the meditation in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:23.960 --> 01:02:31.600
middle so that I'm not continuing to meditate,"&nbsp;
and the meditation retreat manager is like, "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:31.600 --> 01:02:35.360
that won't be allowed. You have to stay. If you&nbsp;
come, you have to stay for the whole thing,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:40.720
that's not really allowing flexibility. So are&nbsp;
there ways that people can titrate the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:45.680
of practice that they're getting within a retreat?&nbsp;
Is there a way to like, "Hey, on Wednesday, we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:45.680 --> 01:02:53.100
have burger night for people who need to increase&nbsp;
the fat intake." That is actually happening now.&nbsp;

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:59.440
Tim Ferriss: The vegetarian diet piece is super&nbsp;
interesting. I mean, I don't know if the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:07.080
acuity is sufficient as a factor, but it makes me&nbsp;
think also, for instance, some of these conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:07.080 --> 01:03:12.320
that are, let's just say, contraindicated&nbsp;
for most psychedelic use, schizophrenia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.320 --> 01:03:16.680
borderline personality disorder, understanding&nbsp;
these are all kind of word salad things taken from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:23.240
the DSM, which is kind of a big question mark&nbsp;
for a lot of reasons. But some of these more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.240 --> 01:03:28.160
for lack of better terms, sort of chaotic&nbsp;
conditions or contraindicated, respond really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:33.680
well to something called metabolic psychiatry. And&nbsp;
Chris Palmer has spoken about this out of Harvard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.680 --> 01:03:39.680
using effectively ketogenic diet but high-fat,&nbsp;
moderate protein, some of these people respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:39.680 --> 01:03:44.240
incredibly well. So if you look though at&nbsp;
the food served at these meditation retreats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:49.760
uniformly effectively the exact polar&nbsp;
opposite, which is kind of interesting.&nbsp;

01:03:49.760 --> 01:03:54.280
Do you meditate? Do you meditate now,&nbsp;
or is that not a practice for you?&nbsp;

01:03:54.280 --> 01:03:56.787
Willoughby Britton: Ooh, that's a long,&nbsp;
long story. It depends on what you —&nbsp;

01:03:56.787 --> 01:03:59.000
Tim Ferriss: All right. Let's do it.
Willoughby Britton: It depends on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:01.060
you mean by meditate.
Tim Ferriss: Okay.&nbsp;

01:04:01.060 --> 01:04:06.560
Willoughby Britton: Meditation, when I&nbsp;
first undertook it with Jack's sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:06.560 --> 01:04:14.240
meditation, it came from a specific tradition&nbsp;
and it had a whole bunch of implicit goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:14.240 --> 01:04:21.040
and values that weren't really clear to me&nbsp;
because I was just suffering. So I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.040 --> 01:04:28.120
I'm just going to do this so I won't feel so much&nbsp;
panic and anxiety and et cetera. So I've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:28.120 --> 01:04:31.240
tens of thousands of hours of meditation. I've&nbsp;
been part of meditation. I've been the expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:40.600
meditator in many brain imaging studies. I've done&nbsp;
my time as a Buddhist meditator, but I've started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:48.507
to understand that there's a whole set of implicit&nbsp;
values that I didn't necessarily choose, so —&nbsp;

01:04:48.507 --> 01:04:51.100
Tim Ferriss: What would be an&nbsp;
example, if you don't mind?&nbsp;

01:04:51.100 --> 01:05:01.080
Willoughby Britton: Well, an example is that the&nbsp;
ideal effect or the goal emotion state is more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:01.080 --> 01:05:04.620
the calm end of things.
Tim Ferriss: Right.&nbsp;

01:05:04.620 --> 01:05:09.000
Willoughby Britton: And as a direct&nbsp;
descendant of General George S. Patton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:14.200
it's not really in my blood to be calm.
Tim Ferriss: More of a border&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:14.200 --> 01:05:18.160
collie, and less of a great Dane. Yeah.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:18.160 --> 01:05:25.280
there's lots of people who their ideal state is&nbsp;
not calm. Yeah. There's lots of implicit values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:31.800
and I think that this is just something,&nbsp;
as meditation is moving into the West and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:31.800 --> 01:05:39.520
kind of mixing with a lot of different cultures&nbsp;
and subcultures and goals and values, can these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:39.520 --> 01:05:47.800
contemplative practices be used for many different&nbsp;
types of goals, rather than ones that were preset&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:47.800 --> 01:05:57.400
as part of a religious practice? And I think that&nbsp;
is happening, but for myself in particular, I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:57.400 --> 01:06:05.800
specific practices that I do, that I feel I have&nbsp;
permission now from myself to modify a practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.800 --> 01:06:11.720
to not just follow the rules the way that I did&nbsp;
before and end up somewhere that I didn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.720 --> 01:06:18.680
to go, but rather to modify things and make sure&nbsp;
that I'm going where I want to go. But that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:18.680 --> 01:06:23.800
requires me to actually sit down with myself&nbsp;
and be like, "Where do you want to go," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.800 --> 01:06:28.320
is another thing that I recommend for people.
Before, Aimee Mann has a song, "Write down what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.320 --> 01:06:34.400
you need before you sign away the deed." Make&nbsp;
sure you know what you want because you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:34.400 --> 01:06:39.240
to get on a highway and it's going to be kind of&nbsp;
fast-tracking you to somewhere, making sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:44.120
you are actually going where you want to go.
Tim Ferriss: I want to know what practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.120 --> 01:06:48.000
that you follow. Not to imply that&nbsp;
anyone should just copy/paste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:48.000 --> 01:06:52.960
right? You've got to know your own needs, but I&nbsp;
am curious where you've landed personally. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:52.960 --> 01:06:57.000
tell me. Where would you like to start?
Willoughby Britton: I want to go to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.000 --> 01:07:01.960
Varieties of Contemplative Experience. This&nbsp;
was the study that took us 10 years, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:01.960 --> 01:07:10.400
came up with 59 categories of meditation-related&nbsp;
challenges and adverse effects, and we categorize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:10.400 --> 01:07:16.320
them into seven different domains, cognitive,&nbsp;
perceptual, affective which is emotional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:22.720
somatic, conative which is motivational, sense&nbsp;
of self, and social. I'm not going to go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.720 --> 01:07:27.360
all 59. People can look them up. They're on&nbsp;
the Cheetah House website under Symptoms.&nbsp;

01:07:27.360 --> 01:07:34.280
But just to give you a sort of basic&nbsp;
overview of what is in each category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:34.280 --> 01:07:40.920
cognitive types of experiences are increased in&nbsp;
thought frequency which you talked about, trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:40.920 --> 01:07:46.760
with executive functioning, so making decisions,&nbsp;
paying attention. The loss of conceptual meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.760 --> 01:07:55.640
structures, which you also mentioned. Probably&nbsp;
the most famous example is the woman who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.640 --> 01:08:02.040
on the way back from retreat, stopped at a red&nbsp;
light, saw the red color, but didn't register&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:05.140
what it meant that it meant stop.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.&nbsp;

01:08:05.140 --> 01:08:06.356
Willoughby Britton: You&nbsp;
can see how that would be —&nbsp;

01:08:06.356 --> 01:08:07.800
Tim Ferriss: Problematic.
Willoughby Britton: Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.800 --> 01:08:09.680
you need your concepts.
Tim Ferriss: Executive function? You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.680 --> 01:08:12.480
need your concepts?
Willoughby Britton: That's the cognitive domain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.480 --> 01:08:18.720
Perceptual, you see perceptual hypersensitivity.&nbsp;
You can also see hallucinations, visions, changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:24.960
in perception, also changes in derealization,&nbsp;
so feeling like you're in a dream or things feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.960 --> 01:08:30.880
flat or cartoon-like. Emotions can go in either&nbsp;
direction which you've already kind of touched on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.880 --> 01:08:37.440
so increases in fear and panic and reexperiencing&nbsp;
of trauma, but also affect of blunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.440 --> 01:08:47.400
so loss of emotions altogether. You can also see,&nbsp;
in the somatic domain, ELSEs, energy-like somatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.400 --> 01:08:50.960
experiences. We have an entire paper on that.
Tim Ferriss: Wait, say that again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.960 --> 01:08:52.960
What does that stand for?
Willoughby Britton: ELSEs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:55.896
energy-like somatic experiences, so —
Tim Ferriss: What was that?&nbsp;

01:08:55.896 --> 01:09:00.200
Willoughby Britton: — experiences of&nbsp;
electricity or voltage going through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:00.200 --> 01:09:07.040
body. We talked about insomnia already and&nbsp;
lots of different kinds of pain syndromes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.040 --> 01:09:13.480
headaches, different kinds of pains.
In terms of conative or motivational,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.480 --> 01:09:23.920
again, insane, just enlightenment or bust kind&nbsp;
of changes in motivation like, "Sorry, honey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:23.920 --> 01:09:28.120
I'm gone. I'm going to go shave my head and become&nbsp;
a monk." We see that all the time. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.120 --> 01:09:34.920
the opposite like complete loss of motivation.&nbsp;
"Why would I do anything?" No goals whatsoever.&nbsp;

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:40.520
The sense of self domain is my personal favorite.&nbsp;
I teach an entire class on it called "Me, Myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:40.520 --> 01:09:49.560
and I," but there's lots of changes in senses of&nbsp;
self and that can range from the narrative self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:49.560 --> 01:09:55.480
the story of me, your identity self, all the&nbsp;
way down to your sense of ownership over your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.480 --> 01:10:03.320
own body, over body sensations, emotions,&nbsp;
thoughts. And then, also down to your sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.320 --> 01:10:12.480
of self-other or self-world boundaries can&nbsp;
dissolve which we have a whole paper on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:12.480 --> 01:10:17.240
as well. Obviously, there's a lot of overlap&nbsp;
with psychedelics with that particular category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:17.240 --> 01:10:21.760
so we can unpack that if you want.
And then, within the social domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.760 --> 01:10:27.600
not surprisingly, if you're having these&nbsp;
experiences, it can be related to all kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:27.600 --> 01:10:33.360
of social consequences. People don't like hearing&nbsp;
about this. They have all sorts of reactions. Wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:33.360 --> 01:10:37.600
till you get your responses to this episode.
Tim Ferriss: I want to talk about the Dalai&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:37.600 --> 01:10:39.560
Lama too, before we get too far.
Willoughby Britton: People don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.560 --> 01:10:45.760
like to hear about this. So a lot of people get&nbsp;
shunned by their communities. Sometimes their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.760 --> 01:10:50.560
teachers get irritated with them, and then&nbsp;
there's also trying to go to work and trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:50.560 --> 01:10:57.720
to explain why you can't function. So, there's&nbsp;
all sorts of social dynamics that go with that.&nbsp;

01:10:57.720 --> 01:11:04.200
That was kind of the quick and dirty overview of&nbsp;
the 59 categories. And I will say that within that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.200 --> 01:11:10.120
particular study, the Varieties of Contemplative&nbsp;
Experience study, we looked at duration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.120 --> 01:11:17.400
symptoms and duration of impairment, and duration&nbsp;
of impairment, the average was one to three years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:17.400 --> 01:11:27.880
So, it was quite a bit of time, and it ranged from&nbsp;
a few days to more than a decade, and I think in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.880 --> 01:11:33.920
your experience, you can kind of understand&nbsp;
why. It could be quite a long time before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.920 --> 01:11:38.947
recover. If you don't happen upon Jack Kornfield&nbsp;
for a while, it could be quite a long time.&nbsp;

01:11:38.947 --> 01:11:47.560
Tim Ferriss: Yes. I won the lottery on that latter&nbsp;
part of things. The duration you're describing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:47.560 --> 01:11:52.480
I just want to draw a couple of parallels.&nbsp;
I mean, you alluded to this, but you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.480 --> 01:12:00.240
take almost everything you just mentioned if&nbsp;
not everything, and map it to adverse events&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:06.480
related to psychedelic experiences, pretty much&nbsp;
everything. I think everything that you mentioned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.480 --> 01:12:11.840
And in terms of duration, there's something that&nbsp;
doesn't get talked about very much also because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.840 --> 01:12:20.880
it is viewed as perhaps risking the cause and&nbsp;
the momentum of the psychedelic renaissance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.880 --> 01:12:24.320
but there's something. It's a bit of a&nbsp;
mouthful. I wish it had a better name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.320 --> 01:12:30.600
but HPPD, Hallucinogen Persisting Perception&nbsp;
Disorder. I know people who've experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.600 --> 01:12:37.200
this where they are effectively hallucinating&nbsp;
on some level for years. I have a family member&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:37.200 --> 01:12:45.480
who experienced this by marriage, and I have a&nbsp;
very close friend who experienced this for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:45.480 --> 01:12:53.040
second half of his undergrad and for several years&nbsp;
afterwards. People can find more on this online.&nbsp;

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:58.600
I suppose we could move into scaffolding,&nbsp;
but I'd like to ask you, are there any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.600 --> 01:13:04.480
particular distinctions, clear differences&nbsp;
between meditation-related adverse events&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.480 --> 01:13:09.960
and psychedelic-related adverse events?
Willoughby Britton: Well, we don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:20.280
know. That's actually a study that we're in the&nbsp;
middle of collecting data on. I'm glad that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.280 --> 01:13:24.640
mentioned that there's a lot of overlaps because&nbsp;
I'm also seeing them, and a lot of people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:32.720
have had both experiences are mentioning that&nbsp;
there is a lot of overlap. The study that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.720 --> 01:13:35.480
conducting is basically looking at all of the&nbsp;
meditation-related, basically seeing how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.480 --> 01:13:42.160
they overlap and whether there are systematic&nbsp;
differences. If I had to guess, I would say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.160 --> 01:13:50.160
the perceptual changes, the HPPD-like changes,&nbsp;
would be more common in the psychedelic world.&nbsp;

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:56.480
I do think there's something neurological going&nbsp;
on there that might be not happening in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.480 --> 01:14:03.120
forms of meditation, but we definitely see visual&nbsp;
snow and lots of — there can be some perceptual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.120 --> 01:14:11.720
changes that are enduring, visual changes that are&nbsp;
enduring. But if you look at the HPPD literature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.720 --> 01:14:17.840
it's actually a lot more than just visual changes.&nbsp;
There's a whole lot of different things going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.840 --> 01:14:22.680
So, I think that's a really interesting question,&nbsp;
not how they compare, but also whether somebody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.680 --> 01:14:27.440
a dual user and whether it's changing that way.
Tim Ferriss: A lot of people are dual users. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.440 --> 01:14:34.480
It's very hard to disambiguate. The resource&nbsp;
I want to recommend, and I can't vouch for it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.480 --> 01:14:41.440
personally because fortunately I do not have&nbsp;
HPPD, but the more people use these compounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.440 --> 01:14:47.080
meaning psychedelics of various types, the more&nbsp;
this is going to become prevalent and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.080 --> 01:14:52.760
need proper addressing. There is a website. It's&nbsp;
perception.foundation, and that's the Perception&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.760 --> 01:15:00.080
Restoration Foundation. They have resources. They&nbsp;
have frequently asked questions about HPPD. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:00.080 --> 01:15:05.880
have a documentary trailer. They also have a&nbsp;
specialist directory. I cannot vouch for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:05.880 --> 01:15:13.040
capabilities of any of the specialists, but a lot&nbsp;
of people who experience this feel the way I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:20.400
in the midst of my silent retreat, which is, "I&nbsp;
am broken. I don't know how to fix this. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:20.400 --> 01:15:29.040
else seems fine. I don't even know what to call&nbsp;
this," and there's a real sense of futility or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:35.280
hopelessness that can come with that. So, suffice&nbsp;
to say, the Perception Restoration Foundation may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.280 --> 01:15:40.120
be a place to start. I can't vouch for it,&nbsp;
but perception.foundation for folks who may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.120 --> 01:15:45.400
want to look that up. What is scaffolding?
Willoughby Britton: For people who come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:50.800
Cheetah House, and let me just back up a little&nbsp;
bit about the Cheetah House sort of origin story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:57.280
I mean, the actual Cheetah House started as a&nbsp;
halfway house for Brown students who wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:57.280 --> 01:16:03.920
become monastics because of our contemplative&nbsp;
studies program. So kids will get meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:03.920 --> 01:16:09.840
training in school as part of college, and they&nbsp;
get so inspired by it that they want to go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.840 --> 01:16:17.280
India and ordained as monks, and going there and&nbsp;
coming back, and living in dorms as a celibate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:24.480
non-intoxicant-taking college students doesn't&nbsp;
really work, so they lived at Cheetah House.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:24.480 --> 01:16:27.080
That was the original place. That was the&nbsp;
original function of Cheetah House was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:27.080 --> 01:16:31.640
support semi-monastic Brown students.
Tim Ferriss: Is it true Cheetah&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:31.640 --> 01:16:34.320
is a play on words, the name?
Willoughby Britton: Cheetah is a play on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.320 --> 01:16:42.840
words. It's a play on the Sanskrit term, citta,&nbsp;
which means, loosely, "mind." When we started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:42.840 --> 01:16:47.800
doing the Varieties of Contemplative Experience&nbsp;
study, we were interviewing a number of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.800 --> 01:16:52.560
who were having meditation-related challenges, and&nbsp;
some of them had just gotten out of the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:52.560 --> 01:17:00.120
and they're like, "I'm 27 and I'm living with&nbsp;
my parents. Can I live with you?" I live at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:00.120 --> 01:17:05.840
Cheetah House, so this is my house. It's a big&nbsp;
Victorian house with lots of floors. So there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:05.840 --> 01:17:12.200
was an entire floor where meditators could live&nbsp;
and kind of recover from whatever was happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.200 --> 01:17:20.240
That was the beginning of Cheetah House.
It is now entirely online, and it is helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:20.240 --> 01:17:24.560
people recover from meditation-related&nbsp;
challenges, and a number of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:24.560 --> 01:17:30.080
they are not able to meditate at all. They're&nbsp;
not able to engage in anything that looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.080 --> 01:17:40.600
meditation. All the trauma cues and associations&nbsp;
are around everything meditation, so sitting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:40.600 --> 01:17:49.400
your eyes closed, sitting on a floor with your&nbsp;
legs crossed, using a soft voice and guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.400 --> 01:17:54.097
all of that triggers the response.
Tim Ferriss: Trigger city.&nbsp;

01:17:54.097 --> 01:17:57.720
Willoughby Britton: So basically, half of the&nbsp;
mental health world just becomes inaccessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:57.720 --> 01:18:01.480
to these people because they go to a&nbsp;
therapist and the therapist is like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:05.760
"Oh, you need to do mindfulness." And they're&nbsp;
like, "No, not mindfulness, please. Anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:05.760 --> 01:18:12.440
but mindfulness." So we have to figure out a&nbsp;
way to help people build their own practice.&nbsp;

01:18:12.440 --> 01:18:23.200
And I think the other piece that I wanted to&nbsp;
unpack with you after I heard your podcast was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:23.200 --> 01:18:29.600
the term ideological power. Ideological&nbsp;
power is the power to control meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.600 --> 01:18:35.520
language, and which concepts are available&nbsp;
and how they're used, the power to interpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:35.520 --> 01:18:42.280
your own or others' experiences, behavior or&nbsp;
feelings, and the power to silence or undermine.&nbsp;

01:18:42.280 --> 01:18:51.520
The important thing here is that trauma is&nbsp;
associated with powerlessness and a lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:51.520 --> 01:19:00.160
options and a lack of agency. So, for people who&nbsp;
have been traumatized by meditation or meditation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.160 --> 01:19:15.360
systems, any kind of system is also a form of&nbsp;
ideological power over them. Even CBT is a system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:15.360 --> 01:19:21.480
any kind of therapy or model is a system,&nbsp;
so that also becomes something that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:21.480 --> 01:19:25.720
not able to work with because it feels&nbsp;
controlling. It feels like it's some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:25.720 --> 01:19:35.320
of value system or model that's being imposed&nbsp;
on them. So how do you work with that? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.320 --> 01:19:39.440
is called person-centered approach.
So if you were my pretend client,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.440 --> 01:19:46.320
I would ask you, "Before you started meditating&nbsp;
or just outside of your meditation life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.320 --> 01:19:51.960
what is something that is a resource for you?"
Tim Ferriss: Just for general well-being? Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:51.960 --> 01:19:54.852
we talking about psychological&nbsp;
well-being as a resource?&nbsp;

01:19:54.852 --> 01:19:57.960
Willoughby Britton: I mean, what I typically&nbsp;
do is actually ask you to look around where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:57.960 --> 01:20:03.680
you are right now and pick something&nbsp;
like a tangible object that's in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.680 --> 01:20:08.947
immediate vicinity. That's something that's&nbsp;
meaningful to you but unrelated to meditation.&nbsp;

01:20:08.947 --> 01:20:13.520
Tim Ferriss: Right. I was going to say, intense&nbsp;
exercise. I've told every girlfriend I've ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.520 --> 01:20:19.640
had, "If you see me trying to sit down and&nbsp;
think my way through a problem for more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:19.640 --> 01:20:24.400
a half hour, just tell me to go lift some&nbsp;
heavy stuff for an hour." It's just like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:24.400 --> 01:20:28.560
"Tell me to get out of the house and go&nbsp;
exercise, and chances are, it'll resolve itself."&nbsp;

01:20:28.560 --> 01:20:36.720
But if I'm looking for something meaningful&nbsp;
that is near me right now, there's quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:36.720 --> 01:20:45.680
bit here around me at the moment. I could say&nbsp;
there's an artist's sketchbook for working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:45.680 --> 01:20:51.040
mostly graphite and ink where I do some drawing.&nbsp;
There is a copy of The Prophet by Kahlil Gibran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:51.040 --> 01:20:56.800
which I read when I hop in the sauna. I'll just&nbsp;
read a couple pages frequently, and then put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.800 --> 01:21:01.200
down. I can keep going, but those are two.
Willoughby Britton: As you're scanning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:01.200 --> 01:21:06.920
environment, you'll notice that when I asked you&nbsp;
notice something that's meaningful, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:06.920 --> 01:21:14.520
salience kind of skipping over certain areas but&nbsp;
glomming onto certain objects, but it's not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:14.520 --> 01:21:20.320
the salience has a different flavor for each one.
So if you were going to think about, "I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.320 --> 01:21:27.880
to take my nervous system in that direction," you&nbsp;
could actually start to chart where you could go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:27.880 --> 01:21:31.760
different options. Just by looking at these&nbsp;
different objects, they're kind of telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:31.760 --> 01:21:35.920
you like, "Oh, this will be activating. This will&nbsp;
be energizing. This will be calming. This will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.920 --> 01:21:42.040
strength inducing." So, pick a direction. Where&nbsp;
would you want your nervous system to go? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:42.040 --> 01:21:47.680
then, pick an object that goes with that.
Tim Ferriss: I'll choose the sketchbook&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.680 --> 01:21:56.000
because that's where I am totally immersed&nbsp;
in something, present tense. I can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:56.000 --> 01:22:02.920
lost in artwork. I don't think about whatever&nbsp;
mistake I made in a conversation yesterday. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:02.920 --> 01:22:09.400
don't think about whatever bullshit I have to&nbsp;
handle tomorrow. I find that very nourishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:09.400 --> 01:22:14.040
in a calming way, so I'll choose that.
Willoughby Britton: As I'm watching you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:14.040 --> 01:22:18.560
I can see your eyes are kind of going somewhere&nbsp;
that's not anywhere in particular, which means you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.560 --> 01:22:23.680
have probably some images that are in your mind.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, maybe. I'm also looking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.680 --> 01:22:29.220
sketchbook that's directly past the&nbsp;
laptop, but we can work with that.&nbsp;

01:22:29.220 --> 01:22:33.000
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. So, maybe more&nbsp;
generally, when you look at the sketchbook&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.000 --> 01:22:36.000
and you're talking about it and you're&nbsp;
thinking about what it can do for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.000 --> 01:22:40.120
what are you noticing that goes with that?
Tim Ferriss: I would say just my sympathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:40.120 --> 01:22:49.400
nervous system seems to downregulate. Breathing&nbsp;
seems to slow a little bit. Hmm, if I'm being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:49.400 --> 01:22:54.560
totally honest, I have a small amount of anxiety&nbsp;
because I was really consistent with my art for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.560 --> 01:23:00.920
a while and I've lapsed for the last two months,&nbsp;
and I feel badly about it, some level of guilt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:00.920 --> 01:23:03.560
but that's not what I'm going for.
Willoughby Britton: Yeah. Let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:03.560 --> 01:23:10.680
just pause there. So, some kind of drop in&nbsp;
your sympathetic nervous system, how is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:10.680 --> 01:23:15.480
manifesting? You said your breathing is starting&nbsp;
to calm down. What else is going with that? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:15.480 --> 01:23:19.720
are other ways that you're noticing —
Tim Ferriss: Some muscular relaxation.&nbsp;

01:23:19.720 --> 01:23:23.960
Willoughby Britton: Any particular area?
Tim Ferriss: Less tension. Yeah. Back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:23.960 --> 01:23:32.240
neck. Those would be most obvious, I would say.&nbsp;
Less fidgety, right? Less bouncing of the foot.&nbsp;

01:23:32.240 --> 01:23:39.960
Willoughby Britton: As you're looking at this&nbsp;
object, this sketchbook, and talking about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.960 --> 01:23:46.160
you're noticing that the sympathetic activation&nbsp;
is decreasing. It's changing your breathing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:46.160 --> 01:23:52.000
There's something happening in your back that's&nbsp;
easing. And as I'm feeding this back to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:52.000 --> 01:23:55.200
what are you noticing that goes with that?
Tim Ferriss: I think it reinforces it. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:56.360 --> 01:24:00.520
not exactly positive reinforcement, but it sounds&nbsp;
like positive reinforcement for the relaxation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:00.520 --> 01:24:05.640
so I'm able to settle into it, I suppose.
Willoughby Britton: This is kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:05.640 --> 01:24:12.840
beginning of scaffolding. This is what it's like&nbsp;
because if I try to say like, "Okay, you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:12.840 --> 01:24:17.840
to focus on your breathing," or "I'm going to&nbsp;
give you an object to focus on," 50 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:17.840 --> 01:24:24.600
people who are given the breath as an object of&nbsp;
meditation find that it is not the optimal — it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.600 --> 01:24:32.920
not a safe object and it can actually induce&nbsp;
anxiety. So, if I have you pick the object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:32.920 --> 01:24:37.800
I couldn't have picked that in a million years.&nbsp;
If I got to wander around your house and be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:37.800 --> 01:24:42.160
"Which thing should I pick," I wouldn't have been&nbsp;
able to pick something. You know immediately. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:49.320
have so much going on, and as a meditator, you're&nbsp;
able to feel into your environment. And there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.320 --> 01:24:55.280
like, "Oh, wow, these have different flavors of&nbsp;
how they're actually impacting me." So I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:55.280 --> 01:25:04.360
spend an hour with you unpacking one particular&nbsp;
