01:25:04.360 --> 01:25:08.240
moving, and it would be certain kinds. You might&nbsp;
be like, "Oh, there's a story that goes with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.240 --> 01:25:14.000
sketchbook," and you might end up on who gave you&nbsp;
the sketchbook, and it's really about that. So, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:14.000 --> 01:25:19.800
kind of go down this rabbit hole together,&nbsp;
and I'm tracking your physiology with mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:19.800 --> 01:25:24.960
and if you're very, very dysregulated and&nbsp;
dissociated, and you're one of those people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:24.960 --> 01:25:32.040
can't feel anything anymore, I can notice very&nbsp;
small changes in your physiology and be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:32.040 --> 01:25:36.400
"Hey, you remember that one thing that you just&nbsp;
said?" Because I saw your eyes brighten up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.400 --> 01:25:45.120
little, I'll be able to specifically amplify&nbsp;
certain things, but I'm following your lead.&nbsp;

01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:50.360
So, as somebody who's been very traumatized&nbsp;
about power dynamics and following any kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:50.360 --> 01:25:57.120
of instructions feels like oppression and&nbsp;
feeling trapped in sort of some system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:57.120 --> 01:26:02.840
if I'm following you and just reflecting back&nbsp;
to you what I'm seeing, that can be a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:02.840 --> 01:26:13.560
bit more palatable to people who — that's kind of&nbsp;
what scaffolding is, and that's what we offer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:13.560 --> 01:26:19.280
meditators who have basically developed&nbsp;
a trauma response to meditation itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:19.280 --> 01:26:23.440
which can really rule out a lot of things. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. That's an elegant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.440 --> 01:26:34.520
workaround/approach. And I would say that I feel&nbsp;
very fortunate that I don't personally, despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:34.520 --> 01:26:40.760
the childhood experience with the abuse, have&nbsp;
that aversion to systems or coaches or teachers, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:40.760 --> 01:26:48.880
think in part, because I'm such a problem student&nbsp;
in the sense that I just will constantly stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:48.880 --> 01:26:55.520
things and question things, and I don't feel like&nbsp;
I need to take anything that is just off the rack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:55.520 --> 01:27:01.920
so to speak. So, I feel very fortunate about that.
Let me return, since I made a promise to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:01.920 --> 01:27:08.200
listeners and to myself that I would, to&nbsp;
your personal practices. What does your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:08.200 --> 01:27:12.740
self-care look like in that department? What&nbsp;
are those practices? Where have you landed?&nbsp;

01:27:12.740 --> 01:27:19.400
Willoughby Britton: Well, I've been doing this&nbsp;
resourcing for a long time, this scaffolding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:20.520 --> 01:27:27.480
and where it landed me, I started to realize&nbsp;
that in some ways not super surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.480 --> 01:27:34.920
but I have a very strong reaction to&nbsp;
living things like animals and plants. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:42.400
